---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="FinGold"
  description="Gold's regime, hedge score, crisis premium and driver panel — real rates, the dollar, financial stress and equity instability — from the FinDynamics gold engine."
  eyebrow="Asset engine"
  heading="FinGold — trust and crisis protection"
  intro="Gold has no cash flow, so there is nothing here to value. This engine models drivers instead: what gold costs to hold, what it is quoted in, and what it is bought against. Everything on this page is read from the live API by your browser."
>
  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="state-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="state-h">Current state</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/assets/gold/state</span>
    </div>
    <div id="gold-state"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="regime-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="regime-h">Regime</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/assets/gold/history?metric=regime_posterior_*</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      Three states, published as a distribution rather than a winner &mdash; a 0.51/0.49 split
      between a hedge bid and a rate headwind is a different statement from a 0.95 hedge bid, and a
      badge alone cannot tell them apart. They are not degrees of one thing either: 1981&ndash;82 was
      a rate headwind and a crisis at the same time.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The posterior comes from two blocks. A Markov regime-switching model on monthly gold returns,
      fitted on an expanding window and frozen between refits, says whether the return process is in
      its violent state. Two logistic gates on the driver panel then say whether that violence is
      accompanied by financial stress (a crisis bid) and whether real rates and the dollar are
      working against gold (a rate headwind). The chain alone cannot do the second job: gold&rsquo;s
      2022 returns averaged +0.04% a month with a 3.6% standard deviation &mdash; statistically
      indistinguishable from a quiet market &mdash; while the real 10-year yield rose 250bp.
    </p>
    <div id="gold-regime" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="hedge-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="hedge-h">Hedge score</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/assets/gold/history?metric=hedge_score</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      0&ndash;100, answering a conditional question: on the days equities are falling, what does gold
      do? Unconditional gold&ndash;equity correlation is close to zero and has been for decades,
      which sounds like a perfect hedge and means almost nothing &mdash; a hedge is not an asset
      that is uncorrelated on average, it is one that is negatively correlated when it matters. So
      the correlation is measured only over days the equity index is more than 5% below its trailing
      peak, then blended 60/40 with the regime posterior, which is the forward-looking half.
    </p>
    <div id="gold-hedge" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="drivers-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="drivers-h">Drivers</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">AssetState.components</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      The real rate is the only genuine cost of holding gold, and the 12-month change is the driver
      that separates 2013 and 2022 from 2011. Before TIPS breakevens existed (2003) it falls back to
      an ex-post rate &mdash; nominal 10y minus trailing CPI &mdash; which is a different quantity
      and is flagged per date rather than blended. A driver that is absent from the information set
      is shown as absent, never as zero.
    </p>
    <div id="gold-drivers" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="signals-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="signals-h">Signals</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">AssetState.signals</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      Direction is the read on gold, not on the direction of the number itself: a rising real rate is
      a headwind even though the number went up, and an elevated crisis premium is supportive of gold
      even though it is bad news for almost everything else.
    </p>
    <div id="gold-signals" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  import '../scripts/gold';
</script>
